# 21. OFFLINE-FIRST ARCHITECTURE AND AUTOMATIC SYNC

This feature is mandatory.

The application must be useful even when the internet connection is unavailable.

## Offline requirements

While offline, the user must be able to:

- View previously cached financial data.
- Add Income.
- Add Expense.
- Add Transfer.
- Add Loan Given.
- Add Loan Received.
- Add Loan Repayment.
- Add Savings Transactions.
- Add Assets and Liabilities.
- Edit supported records.
- Search cached transactions.
- Continue using Quick Add.

## Firestore offline persistence

Use the current modular Firebase Web SDK.

Configure persistent local Firestore caching before other Firestore operations.

Use multi-tab persistence where supported.

Gracefully handle unavailable browsers or persistence failures and clearly communicate the resulting limitation.

Do not assume that Web offline persistence is enabled automatically.

## Local attachment storage

Store new offline receipt images and other attachments in IndexedDB until they can be uploaded to Firebase Storage.

Keep attachment upload status.

Do not discard local files before successful cloud upload.

## Automatic synchronization

When connectivity returns:

1. Detect network recovery.
2. Allow Firestore to synchronize its pending writes.
3. Upload queued attachments.
4. Update sync statuses.
5. Refresh relevant summary calculations.
6. Display any synchronization errors.
7. Retry failed operations safely.

Use stable UUIDs for client-created records.

Use unique operation IDs and idempotent processing to prevent duplicate financial entries.

Display these states:

- Synced
- Pending Sync
- Syncing
- Sync Failed
- Conflict Needs Review

Never show an unsynced transaction as successfully backed up.

## Conflict management

This application may be used on Desktop and Mobile simultaneously.

Do not silently overwrite financial data when two devices edit the same record.

Use revision numbers, immutable revision/audit records, and a conflict review mechanism.

If two devices create competing revisions based on the same prior revision:

- Preserve both versions.
- Identify the conflicting fields.
- Show the user both versions.
- Allow the user to choose the correct version.
- Record the resolution in Audit History.

Use separate event or revision IDs rather than relying entirely on a single last-write-wins mutable document.

Where offline support is required, prefer write batches and queued writes. Do not make a critical offline workflow depend on an online-only Firestore transaction.

## PWA

Provide:

- Web App Manifest
- Service Worker
- App Shell Caching
- Offline Fallback
- Installability
- Cache Version Management

Do not cache private financial data in a publicly accessible cache.

Clear sensitive local cached data when the user explicitly selects the corresponding secure sign-out / data clearing operation, while warning about pending unsynced records.

